WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
A queue message should be a contract, not arbitrary JSON that every worker interprets differently.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:13.720
The contract should define message type, version, required fields, allowed types, size limits, formats, and meaning.

3
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.220
When the contract is vague, consumers quietly make their own assumptions.

4
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:28.400
Those assumptions become security risks when a producer changes a field used for tenant routing, authorization, file paths, payment amounts, or workflow state.

5
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.280
Validate payloads before using them in business logic.

6
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:40.620
Reject unsupported message types, unknown versions, malformed payloads, oversized messages, and unexpected structures.

7
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:55.760
Treat user IDs, tenant IDs, object keys, file paths, URLs, email addresses, roles, amounts, scopes, and workflow state as untrusted until verified against authoritative data.

8
00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:02.420
A message can be valid according to the schema and still request an action the user or tenant should not be allowed to perform.

9
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.220
Versioning matters because queue systems often evolve gradually.

10
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.000
A producer may deploy before every consumer is updated.

11
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.580
A worker may process messages created by an older version of the API.

12
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:19.800
A schema change may add a sensitive field or alter the meaning of a status.

13
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:27.100
Consumers should fail safely when they receive unsupported versions rather than silently applying old assumptions to new data.

14
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.360
Producers and consumers need clear contract expectations.

15
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.600
Validation failures need an intentional path.

16
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.100
Some failures can be rejected and counted.

17
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.360
Others should be routed to a dead-letter queue or investigation process.

18
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:50.580
Logs should capture the message type, version, validation category, and safe correlation ID without dumping full sensitive payloads.

19
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:59.000
Schema validation supports security, but it does not replace authorization, ownership checks, tenant checks, or business-state checks.
